WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.718
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of "Cloud Performance Atlas,"

00:00:02.718 --> 00:00:05.140
we take a Compute Engine's
disk options for a spin.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:07.151
Will their performance
leave us dizzy?

00:00:07.151 --> 00:00:08.109
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:11.050 --> 00:00:14.530
When we talk about the "cloud,"
we tend to over index on things

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:17.780
like "compute" and "network,"
because quite frankly,

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.790
those are the things
that the "cloud" can do,

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:22.180
which the machine sitting
under your desk can't.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:25.420
But with this focus, we tend
to forget something-- disks.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:28.240
See, all that data you're
processing has to go somewhere.

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.615
And on Google Cloud Platform,
choosing the right type of disk

00:00:31.615 --> 00:00:33.490
can make a huge difference
in the performance

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:34.870
of your application.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:37.360
As such, let's take a look
at what type of disk options

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:40.060
are available and what their
performance looks like.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.370
In Google Cloud Platform,
there are two main types

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:46.030
of disks-- local SSDs
and persistent disks.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.340
Local SSDs are pretty
much what you'd expect,

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.170
drives that are
physically attached to

00:00:50.170 --> 00:00:51.820
your virtual machine instance.

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:55.810
So when your VM spins down,
so does the data on the disk.

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:57.460
Persistent disks,
on the other hand,

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:00.790
are network storage devices
that act like a local storage

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:02.560
device with the
upside being that they

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.560
can persist even after your
VM stops and can even be

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.960
reattached to a new VM later.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.300
And on the persistent
disk side, it's

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.000
worth noting that there's
two flavors, standard

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.090
and SSD, which behave
pretty much the same

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:19.190
except one is a lot
faster than the other.

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:21.520
Now, I understand that every
application is different

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.470
and has unique
needs, which might

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:25.670
make you choose one of these
options from the other.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:28.630
But from my perspective, I
care about the best performance

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.250
for the best price.

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:32.980
To get a sense of this,
a small benchmark tests

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:34.990
shows us how each of
the disks performed,

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:37.630
measuring the number of
IO operations per second

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:39.670
per gigabyte of data.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:42.120
Directly, we see a
very clear trend.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:46.120
Local SSDs outperform both
types of persistent disks, which

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.850
completely makes sense, given
that local SSDs are physically

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:49.930
connected to the VM.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:51.850
So we should see better perf.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:55.660
This graph also shows us that as
far as the persistent disks go,

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:58.540
the SSD variant outperforms
their standard counterparts

00:01:58.540 --> 00:01:59.780
quite easily.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:02.080
But this doesn't really
tell the full story,

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.600
which is why I like to break
up the graph between throughput

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:07.810
and IOPS to help figure out
what disk is the best use

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.090
case for what
application scenario.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:13.300
For example, boot disk
volumes and bulk storage

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:16.480
are great matches for
standard persistent disks

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.690
because they require very
low IOPS and low throughput.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:21.740
Basically, you just want
to occupy the cheapest,

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:23.440
most reliable space available.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:25.480
Likewise, streaming
IO works best

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:28.510
with standard persistent disks
since the cost per throughput

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:30.640
is generally a little better.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:34.540
Now, relational databases
tend to be more IOPS heavy.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.190
And while smaller
instances might

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.604
be able to run on
standard PD, you really

00:02:38.604 --> 00:02:40.270
want your production
deployments running

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:43.150
on some sort of SSD flavor disk.

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:46.610
File servers mostly
fall in the SSD-PD camp,

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:48.580
since they can either be
more streaming or more

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.170
transactional, depending on
what the clients are doing.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:53.590
And finally, high
performance scratch disks

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:56.050
tend to perform
best on local SSD,

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:58.000
which is also an ideal
use case for things

00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.786
like Hadoop deployments.

00:02:59.786 --> 00:03:02.410
Now, it's worth reiterating that
each of the three disk options

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:04.060
have various types
of configurations

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:07.120
that can change how they perform
under different workloads,

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:09.679
which is why we've got
more videos on the way.

00:03:09.679 --> 00:03:12.220
So make sure you check out the
rest of the "Cloud Performance

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:14.600
Atlas" content, because when
it comes to performance,

00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:15.940
every millisecond counts.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

